  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening indigenous and workers rights in Mexico</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The number of poor people in Mexico increased to 80% and the number of extremely poor to 30%. Mexico continues to have one of the highest inequality rates, with growing labour and precarious family conditions for the majority of people. In a context of persecution and militarisation, there is a growing irrational hardship against the poor.

The indigenous peoples of M&#xe9;xico have been excluded from national development processes for centuries, with  poverty, inequality and marginalisation prevailing amongst indigenous peoples. Levels of corruption and inefficiency in governmental offices (Prosecutor&#x2019;s Office, Social Security, Ministry of Labour, Agrarian Ombudsman) have continued to rise and actions of social movements are increasingly criminalised, with cases of death threats, disappearances and imprisonment of human rights activists. The presence of the army in Chiapas has intensified, generating widespread insecurity and tension within the indigenous communities. Ostensibly, this increased presence is to combat organised crime and gain control of indigenous territories rich in natural resources and ripe for exploitation under the Plan Puebla Panam&#xe1; (now called Plan Mesoamerica).  At community level the problems are broad based, with growing divisions between communities, the legalization of forced evictions, an increase in alcoholism and migration, rising levels of consumerism, all of which overtake environmental concerns and awareness, electricity failures (radio); and the imposition of political party campaigns on local radio spots. The assistential character of public social programmes doesn&#xb4;t allow minimum conditions for subsistence; the narcotraffic has triggered a constant violence; the economic crisis is provocking massive migrations at the same time migrants return due to lack of job opportunities in the north. Chiapas and Veracruz are the Mexican states with second and fourth highest levels of marginalisation in Mexico.

The global economic crisis and the fall in value of the Mexican peso against the dollar and the euro have meant the closure and bankruptcy of a number of factories and businesses, with significant job losses. This job insecurity is resulting in systematic violations to Labour Human Rights and to higher vulnerability for workers, who have to accept precarious working conditions due to the risk of losing their jobs.
  
This project aims to contribute to defend the rights to a dignified life, to the identity and unity of indigenous communities and peoples in three zones of southeast Mexico and to respect and defend labour human rights and to strengthen organisations of workers in the extractives and electronic sectors.</narrative>
  </description>
